IPv4 Addressing – Octets and Dotted Decimal Notation

Overview

IPv4 is the addressing system used to uniquely identify devices on a network.
Each device connected to the internet or a local network is assigned an IPv4 address.

---

IPv4 Address Length

An IPv4 address is 32 bits long.

Example of a 32-bit binary IPv4 address:

11010001101001011100100000000001

Binary format is difficult for humans to read and remember.

---

Dividing the Address into Octets

To make the address readable, the 32 bits are divided into four groups of 8 bits.

Each group of 8 bits is called an octet.

Example:

11010001.10100101.11001000.00000001

Structure:

[8 bits] . [8 bits] . [8 bits] . [8 bits]

Total:

8 + 8 + 8 + 8 = 32 bits

---

Binary to Decimal Conversion

Each octet is converted from binary to decimal to make it easier for humans to read.

Binary| Decimal
11010001| 209
10100101| 165
11001000| 200
00000001| 1

Final dotted-decimal IPv4 address:

209.165.200.1

---

Range of Each Octet

Each octet contains 8 bits.

The number of possible values is:

2^8 = 256

So each octet ranges from:

0 – 255

---

IPv4 Address Format

IPv4 addresses follow this format:

[0–255].[0–255].[0–255].[0–255]

Example IPv4 addresses:

192.168.1.1
10.0.0.1
172.16.0.1

---

Purpose of IPv4

IPv4 addresses allow devices to identify and communicate with each other on a network.

Example network:

Device| Example IP Address
Router| 192.168.1.1
Laptop| 192.168.1.10
Smartphone| 192.168.1.11
Server| 209.165.200.1

Without IP addressing, devices would not know where to send data.

---

Key Points

- IPv4 address length: 32 bits
- Divided into 4 octets
- Each octet = 8 bits
- Each octet range: 0 – 255
- Written in dotted-decimal notation

Example:

209.165.200.1